      "instructions": "1-6: Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.\n7: Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for your answer.\n8: Write NO MORE THAN THREE WORDS for your answer.\n9-10: Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Amrita Cole: Good afternoon, this is Northbank Hearing Centre. I will note your musical work and current concern before we choose filters, impressions and collection. I will start with the legal name for your hearing record. What surname should I enter?\nDylan Mercer: The surname is Mercer, M-E-R-C-E-R. My band’s booking uses a stage name, so please use the legal surname here.\nAmrita Cole: Tell me about your main instrument and the kind of ensemble in which you usually rehearse.\nDylan Mercer: I play violin in a chamber group, although I occasionally work beside amplified percussion. Most rehearsals are acoustic, but festival work occasionally places me close to amplified percussion.\nAmrita Cole: That pattern helps us choose a filter, alongside sensible distance whenever a particularly loud source is present. You noted uneven fatigue. Which side has been giving you more concern?\nDylan Mercer: The left ear feels more fatigued after rehearsal; the right one has not caused the same problem. Given that most of my work is acoustic, which of the three filter strengths would you recommend?\nAmrita Cole: For mainly acoustic work, the medium-strength filter is suitable; the strongest option would remove more detail than you want.\nDylan Mercer: My current foam plugs make speech sound muffled, which is why I sometimes remove one during tuning.\nAmrita Cole: A filtered custom pair should preserve more useful detail, but both plugs need to remain in place for balanced protection. The mould colour will not change performance. Which finish would you prefer?\nDylan Mercer: I will choose clear moulds rather than blue, because they are less visible on stage. Will changing the mould colour later alter the filter itself?\nAmrita Cole: No, colour is cosmetic; changing attenuation would require a different filter component.\nDylan Mercer: Which date have you booked for the hearing check before the impressions are taken?\nAmrita Cole: The hearing check is booked for the sixth of May, with impressions immediately afterwards if the result is suitable.\nDylan Mercer: Is there anything I should avoid putting in my ears on the morning of that appointment?\nAmrita Cole: Avoid ear drops on the morning of the appointment unless a clinician has advised them.\nDylan Mercer: Which piece of my stage equipment should I bring so you can check physical compatibility?\nAmrita Cole: Bring the stage monitor earpiece so we can check that both devices sit comfortably together.\nDylan Mercer: I will bring my earlier hearing-test details and the earpiece I use during performances.\nAmrita Cole: Excellent. We can compare fit and balance with your own equipment during the collection appointment.\nDylan Mercer: How long does your laboratory normally need to complete a pair?\nAmrita Cole: Production normally takes two weeks, although a remake would extend that.\nDylan Mercer: When I collect them, where will you check the final seal and left-right balance?\nAmrita Cole: The final seal is checked here at the clinic during collection, not remotely at rehearsal.\nDylan Mercer: I have never used custom plugs, so I may need practice inserting them without a mirror.\nAmrita Cole: We teach that at collection and mark the cases clearly for the left and right sides.\nDylan Mercer: Can I clean the moulds after every performance without damaging the removable filters?\nAmrita Cole: Yes. Take the filters out first, wipe the moulds as demonstrated, and let them dry fully before reassembly. I will confirm the hearing check and impression visit by email as well. Please bring the performance earpiece and your previous test information.",
