      "transcript": "Dr Helen Shaw: For the seminar-layout study, participation must be observable without changing the teaching task. Let us settle the outcome, controls and coding procedure.\nKeira Moss: The main outcome should be how speaking turns are distributed across the group, not simply the total number of comments.\nDr Helen Shaw: The project compares room layouts, not the quality of the students’ academic opinions.\nKeira Moss: The coding manual distinguishes an interruption from supportive overlap, since brief agreement should not automatically count as competitive speech.\nTomislav Reed: Follow-up interviews occur after the second session, when each student can compare two arrangements from direct experience.\nTomislav Reed: If one group has more students, its members have fewer opportunities to speak, so group size must remain constant.\nKeira Moss: All groups will discuss the same short case study and receive the same preparation time.\nTomislav Reed: Microphones remain in identical positions relative to the table centre, and a short test phrase checks audibility before each session.\nDr Helen Shaw: More speech will not automatically mean better discussion; the analysis reports distribution without claiming that quantity proves learning quality.\nDr Helen Shaw: Audio lets us identify speakers and overlaps while avoiding unnecessary images of participants, which makes the procedure less intrusive.\nTomislav Reed: The tutor will use a written opening and closing instruction so one group is not given a warmer introduction.\nDr Helen Shaw: The tutor follows a fixed prompt script and waits the same number of seconds before intervening after silence.\nKeira Moss: The facilitator avoids calling on named quiet students, as targeted invitations would change the participation pattern being observed.\nKeira Moss: In the pilot, the tutor waited different lengths of time before prompting, so some groups had more opportunity to fill a silence themselves.\nDr Helen Shaw: Speaking time and turn count will be analysed separately because one long contribution should not be treated as many turns.\nKeira Moss: If a student is absent, that group session is rescheduled rather than analysed with fewer available speakers.\nTomislav Reed: Transcripts replace every name with a study code before the researchers calculate speaking duration and turn counts.\nTomislav Reed: We will rotate the layouts across seminar groups rather than letting every group encounter the circular arrangement first.\nKeira Moss: Participants will choose a study code, allowing the research team to connect both sessions without placing names on transcripts.\nTomislav Reed: Seats are allocated by study code because letting friends cluster could alter turn exchange independently of the furniture arrangement.\nDr Helen Shaw: A rehearsal recording will calibrate the coding rules, but its participants and data will not enter the main comparison.\nDr Helen Shaw: We should recruit second-year students, whose seminar experience is established but still broadly comparable.\nDr Helen Shaw: Two coders will independently score a sample of recordings, then discuss definitions where their classifications differ.\nKeira Moss: Eight students per group will allow several voices without making reliable speaker identification unmanageable.\nDr Helen Shaw: The three layouts will be a circle, a U shape and paired rectangular tables, each assembled from the same movable furniture.\nKeira Moss: Room bookings use the same teaching space and matched afternoon slots to limit variation in acoustics, fatigue and corridor noise.\nTomislav Reed: Observers will record interruptions separately from supportive overlaps, because the two may affect participation differently.\nKeira Moss: Students receive one case text forty-eight hours before class, so unfamiliar reading cannot explain a quieter contribution in one condition.\nTomislav Reed: The observer notes a fire alarm, late arrival or unexpected room entry, because these events may interrupt only one condition.\nDr Helen Shaw: Before the first seminar, a brief scale will record speaking confidence so we can check whether one layout begins with a more confident group.\nTomislav Reed: Keira will prepare the confidence scale, I will test the turn-coding sheet, and Dr Shaw will approve the rotated timetable.\nTomislav Reed: Numbered place cards let observers identify voices from the audio without attaching names to the coding spreadsheet.\nDr Helen Shaw: Participation is voluntary, contributes nothing to grades and can be withdrawn before the anonymised data are combined.\nKeira Moss: The follow-up interview will last ten minutes and ask what made it easier or harder to enter the discussion.\nDr Helen Shaw: A speaking turn begins when a student takes the floor and ends after a meaningful pause or another speaker gains it.\nKeira Moss: The confidence scale is completed before students see the first layout, preventing the furniture itself from shaping the baseline response.\nTomislav Reed: The seminar study now separates layout, speaker confidence and tutor behaviour while keeping the discussion task consistent across groups.",
