      "instructions": "31-36: Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS AND/OR A NUMBER for each answer.\n37-40: Write NO MORE THAN TWO WORDS for each answer.",
      "transcript": "Professor Evan Marr: Today I will use a coastal-forecasting simulation from our methods course. Its storm track and impact figures belong to the exercise, so focus on how the forecast develops.\nProfessor Evan Marr: For the exercise, we use atmospheric river for a narrow zone carrying concentrated water vapour, not liquid water in the sky.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Our satellite, radar and aircraft records sample different parts of the corridor, comparing offshore shape, inland precipitation and changing intensity.\nProfessor Evan Marr: In the forecast notes, integrated vapour transport combines wind and moisture through a vertical slice, making it more informative than surface humidity alone.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Relative to its length, the corridor is narrow, although its boundaries shift through time.\nProfessor Evan Marr: In these notes, the term covers atmospheric transport over thousands of kilometres, even though the corridor remains comparatively narrow.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Moisture is carried by strong winds through a deep layer, with supply and loss changing as the system travels.\nProfessor Evan Marr: For our forecast scenario, imagine the corridor weakening offshore, then intensifying again near the coast.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Communicators can describe confidence in broad timing while remaining less certain about the narrow band of heaviest rain.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Much of the moisture comes from evaporation over the ocean and may be supplemented along the route.\nProfessor Evan Marr: In the simulation's next stage, landfall is not automatically damaging; the region may depend on the event for water supply.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Follow a four-step forecast chain: oceanic moisture, atmospheric transport, terrain and local exposure.\nProfessor Evan Marr: On the worksheet, rising terrain causes orographic uplift as the air moves inland.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Impacts vary with antecedent soil moisture, freezing level, terrain and the timing of other storms.\nProfessor Evan Marr: The model shelters the leeward side of its mountains, contrasting its two regions.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Cooling promotes condensation and can produce intense precipitation on windward slopes.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Moist air rises over hills and mountains, forming condensation, clouds and precipitation on the windward side.\nProfessor Evan Marr: The same precipitation becomes hazardous when catchments are saturated or freezing levels rise over deep existing snow.\nProfessor Evan Marr: In the track test, shifting the forecast corridor moves the heaviest rainfall from one named basin to another.\nProfessor Evan Marr: The next sequence sends several atmospheric rivers through the study area, increasing cumulative rain and snow totals.\nProfessor Evan Marr: We now turn to the forecast record: sparse offshore observations combine with model guidance, leaving uncertainty before landfall.\nProfessor Evan Marr: In cold conditions, the event may add substantially to the snowpack rather than falling entirely as rain.\nProfessor Evan Marr: River response occurs after atmospheric forcing, and the lag differs with basin size, slope and existing water content.\nProfessor Evan Marr: In the forecast case, the exact landfall location determines which labelled catchment receives the heaviest precipitation.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Forecast models differ in track and timing, and those differences propagate into river forecasts and reservoir decisions.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Reservoir operators balance flood space against water supply, using updated forecasts rather than one decision made at the start of the storm.\nProfessor Evan Marr: For the reservoir plan, revisit the water-management decision whenever the observations or guidance are updated.\nProfessor Evan Marr: The worksheet asks students to estimate the event's duration as well as its peak strength before comparing outcomes.\nProfessor Evan Marr: For this lecture, my recommendation is to state both the most likely forecast and a credible alternative, so listeners can see what remains uncertain.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Time-integrated vapour transport helps forecasters describe moisture delivered over a period, not only a single peak.\nProfessor Evan Marr: The final block compares impacts across the study area. Its agricultural zone may gain soil moisture while transport and urban drainage face disruption.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Recently burned slopes may produce rapid runoff because vegetation and soil structure have been altered.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Warm events can raise the snowline and add rain to existing snow, increasing runoff more quickly than a colder event.\nProfessor Evan Marr: On the worksheet, vapour transport is the horizontal flow of water vapour through the model atmosphere.\nProfessor Evan Marr: A landfalling event may be beneficial when reservoirs are low and soils can absorb rain without producing damaging runoff.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Burn scars and urban surfaces can produce rapid runoff, whereas forested or dry soils may respond more slowly.\nProfessor Evan Marr: For the planning task, compare three plausible tracks and rainfall totals before choosing a response for the training town.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Freezing level is crucial because rain falling onto snow has different runoff consequences from new snow stored at altitude.\nProfessor Evan Marr: In the debrief, each group explains how its chosen threshold altered the response; the aim is to compare decisions, not declare a universal threshold.\nProfessor Evan Marr: For the public bulletin, communicate an uncertainty range instead of presenting one track and rainfall total as certain.\nProfessor Evan Marr: To close the simulation, the corridor gains moisture from the ocean, mixes with surrounding air and loses water through precipitation.\nProfessor Evan Marr: After the event, the class compares the forecast with the course observations to locate errors in transport, landfall or terrain response.\nProfessor Evan Marr: Ensemble forecasts sample multiple plausible developments, producing a distribution rather than one supposedly exact future path.\nProfessor Evan Marr: My final recommendation is to update track, duration and uncertainty whenever new observations alter the range of local impacts.",
